AB  - This amicus curiae brief argues that the City of Norfolk's warrantless operation of its mass surveillance Automated License Plate Reader (ALPR) system constitutes an unreasonable search violating the Fourth Amendment. Modern ALPR systems gather extensive location data, enabling law enforcement to infer identities and monitor routines, thereby infringing on privacy rights. Furthermore, AI-enabled ALPRs perpetuate discriminatory predictive policing by relying on biased data and limiting transparency, which risks reviving general warrant powers and undermining democratic principles.
