AB  - Because wireless sensor networks (WSNs) have been demonstrated to be an effective technology for gathering information about the physical world, they have been put to use in a wide variety of applications, such as the monitoring of temperature, radiation, the movement of liquids, and many others. Because of the characteristics of this type of technology and the fact that these systems are more susceptible to being attacked, the security measures that must be implemented for them must be approached in a unique manner. Because it helps sensors work together more effectively, decision making in a WSN is a key component for the successful completion of particular activities. Trust management systems could potentially play a significant role in this process in order to provide assistance. Emerging networks known as Wireless Sensor Networks (WSNs) are currently being utilized in a wide number of applications, including remote sensing imaging, military, healthcare, and traffic monitoring, among other things. This presents a dilemma for the security industry. In addition, sensor networks are believed to be the heart of both the Internet of Things (IoT) and smart cities. The proposed methodology has a trust based routing system through a main goal on packed dropping attacks. These attacks take place when biased sensors try to limit the network. Results depicts improper performance of model is 60% even when 25% or less lodes in number which performed in an unacceptable behavior.  © 2022 IEEE.
